          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong sufficiently cohesive topos
            is a cohesive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            that has enough connected objects in the sense that every object embeds into a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+object"} connected object
          %p
            This can be viewed as a strong form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesiveness
            in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} Lawvere's
            axiomatic approach to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gros+toposes"} gros toposes
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Law86"} 1986
          %h2#terminology Terminology
          %p
            Sufficient cohesion is a relative concept and requires minimally the presence of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential geometric morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{S}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+quadruple"} adjoint quadruple
            between toposes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!\dashv p^* \dashv p_* \dashv p^!\;\colon\;\mathcal{S}\to\mathcal{E}
          %p
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^!
            (and hence
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^\ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful"} fully faithful
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!
            preserves
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
          %p
            This (and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            in particular) is called a ‘cohesive topos’ (over
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
            %em ‘weakly cohesive topos’
            in the present entry - a sufficiently cohesive topos in this context corresponds to the three axioms (0-2) for a ‘gros topos’ in Lawvere (
            %a{:href => "#Law86"}> 1986
            ) where the concept of sufficient cohesion was considered for the first time.
          %p
            A weakly cohesive topos is called
            %em pre-cohesive
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            furthermore satisfies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nullstellensatz"} Nullstellensatz
            i.e. the canonical map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta:p_\ast\to p_!
            is an epimorphism. This is the situation explored in Menni (
            %a{:href => "#Menni14a"}> 2014a
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Menni14b"} 2014b
          %p
            A pre-cohesive topos that moreover satisfies the
            %em continuity principle
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi p
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(X^{p^\ast(Y)})\simeq p_!(X)^{Y}
            natural in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in\mathcal{E}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\in\mathcal{S}
            %em ‘cohesive’
            in Lawvere (
            %a{:href => "#Law07"}> 2007
            ) where the term ‘sufficently cohesive’ occurs for the first time although the notion is defined in a more restricted environment than the earlier papers (cf. Lawvere
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Law86"} 1986
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Law92"} 1992
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Law99"} 1999
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          #Contractible_object.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              An object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              in a weakly cohesive topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{S}
              is called
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+space"} contractible
              if for every object
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\in\mathcal{E}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(X^Y)=1
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              In particular, a contractible object is connected:
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(X)=p_!(X^1)=1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^Y
              preserves
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              in any Cartesian closed category, the terminal object is contractible in any weakly cohesive topos.
          #Sufficient_Cohesion.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A weakly cohesive topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{S}
              is called
              %em sufficiently cohesive
              if the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega\in\mathcal{E}
              is contractible i.e.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(\Omega^X)=1
              for every object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in\mathcal{E}
              or, in other words, if all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+object"} power objects
              are connected.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Since in general, every object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              embeds into its power object via the singleton map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↣
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\}:X\rightarrowtail\Omega^X
              it follows that in a sufficiently cohesive topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              every object embeds into a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+object"} connected object
              i.e.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              has
              = succeed "." do
                %em enough connected objects
            %p
              Furthermore, since in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+closed+category"} Cartesian closed category
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi Y
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Z
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Z
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi Z
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X^Y)^Z\simeq X^{(Y\times Z)}\simeq X^{(Z\times Y)}\simeq (X^Z)^Y
            %p
              one sees that in a sufficiently cohesive topos power objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^X
              are not only connected but even contractible:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!((\Omega^X)^Z)=p_!(\Omega^{X\times Z})=1
            %p
              and hence it follows that in a sufficiently cohesive topos
              = succeed "." do
                %em every object embeds into a contractible object
            %p
              Conversely, if every power object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^X
              embeds into a connected object then the power objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^X
              will be connected themselves by proposition
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#connected_retract"}
              below since power objects are injective in general. Whence a (weakly) cohesive topos is sufficiently cohesive iff every object embeds into a connected object iff every object embeds into a contractible object. The last formulation is taken as the definition of sufficient cohesion in Lawvere (
              %a{:href => "#Law07"}> 2007
              ).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              The definition of sufficient cohesion by ‘having enough contractible objects’ (Lawvere
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Law07"} 2007
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive categories
              that lack a subobject classifier. The definition by ‘having enough connected objects’ would be feasible for merely
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+category"} distributive categories
              that are not necessarily Cartesian closed (cf. Lawvere
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Law91a"} 1991
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Law92"} 1992
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Law91a"} 1991
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p Let us first record some easy but useful facts concerning the interplay between connectedness and constancy.
          %p
            Recall that in a category with a terminal object a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c:X\to Y
            is called
            %em constant
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            factors through the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                  %mi Y
                  %mo =
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo !
                        %mi X
                  %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mo *
                  %mi Y
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\overset{c}{\to} Y=X\overset{!_X}{\to}1\overset{c_\ast}{\to} Y\quad .
          #constant_identity.num_prop
            %h6#observation Observation
            %p
              An object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a terminal object iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_X
              is constant.
          %p
            %strong> Proof
            \. “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇐
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Leftarrow
            ”: By assumption
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mo !
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_X=(id_X)_\ast\circ !_X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (id_X)_\ast
            there exists for every object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            at least one map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z\to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is some map
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z\to X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mo !
                    %mi X
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mo !
                    %mi Z
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f=id_X\circ f=(id_X)_\ast\circ !_X\circ f=(id_X)_\ast\circ !_Z\quad .
          %p
            But the righthand side does not depend on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            hence it is the only map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z\to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          #constant_homotopy.num_prop
            %h6#observation_2 Observation
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be an object in a weakly cohesive topos and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c:X\to X
              be a constant endomap such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(c)=p_!(id_X)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is connected i.e.
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(X)=1
          %p
            %strong> Proof
            \. Since by assumption
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(1)=1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(c)
            and, accordingly,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(id_X)=id_{p_!(X)}
            are constants.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          #connected_retract.num_prop
            %h6#observation_3 Observation
            %p In a weakly cohesive topos, retracts of connected objects are connected themselves.
          %p
            %strong> Proof
            \. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a retract of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(Y)=1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_X
            factors as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↣
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\rightarrowtail Y\to X
            and applying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!
            shows that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_{p_!(X)}
            factors through the terminal object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(Y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p
            Since in general, injective objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            are retracts of the objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that they embed into because such inclusions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo ↣
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I\rightarrowtail X
            factor through
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_I
            by injectivity, it follows that in a weakly cohesive topos
            %em injective objects that embed into a connected object are connected
            themselves.
          %p
            In particular,
            %em all
            injective objects are connected in a sufficiently cohesive topos. Since power objects are injective in general one gets the converse as well:
          #sufficient_injective.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A weakly cohesive topos is sufficiently cohesive iff all injective objects are connected.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ▪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p We can replace ‘connected’ with ‘contractible’ in this proposition as the following propositions show.
          #contractinjective.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              be a topos. Then all injective objects are connected iff all injective objects are contractible.
          %p
            %strong> Proof
            \. “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            ”: by the following lemma.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          #exponential_injective.num_prop
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              be an injective object in a topos
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^X
              is injective for any object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              , or in other words, exponentials
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (_-)^X
              preserve injective objects.
          %p
            %strong> Proof
            \. (cf. at
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+object#Adjuncts_Injectives"} injective object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo :
                  %mi S
                  %mo ↣
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m:S\rightarrowtail B
            the induced function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi m
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m^\ast:Hom(B,I^X)\to Hom(S,I^X)
            is onto. From the exponential adjunction one has the following commutative diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi B
                          %mo ,
                          %msup
                            %mi I
                            %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi m
                                %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi S
                          %mo ,
                          %msup
                            %mi I
                            %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≃
                          %mo ∥
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∥
                          %mo ≃
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ×
                          %mi B
                          %mo ,
                          %mi I
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi m
                              %mo ×
                              %msub
                                %mi id
                                %mi X
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ×
                          %mi S
                          %mo ,
                          %mi I
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Hom(B,I^X) &amp;\overset{m^\ast}{\rightarrow} &amp; Hom(S,I^X)
                  \\\
                  \\simeq \parallel&amp; &amp;\parallel
                  \\simeq
                  \\\
                  Hom(X\times B,I) &amp;\underset{(m\times id_X)^\ast}{\rightarrow} &amp;Hom(X\times S,I)
                  }
          %p
            whence it suffices to show that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi S
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\times id_X:S\times X\to B\times X
            is a mono since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is injective. But this is easy to see:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi m
                          %mo ×
                          %msub
                            %mi id
                            %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∘
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %msub
                            %mi f
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi f
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi m
                          %mo ×
                          %msub
                            %mi id
                            %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∘
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %msub
                            %mi g
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi g
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi m
                          %mo ∘
                          %msub
                            %mi f
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi f
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi m
                          %mo ∘
                          %msub
                            %mi g
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi g
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  (m\times id_X)\circ \langle f_1,f_2\rangle &amp;= (m\times id_X)\circ\langle g_1,g_2\rangle
                  \\\
                  \\langle m\circ f_1, f_2\rangle &amp;=\langle m\circ g_1,g_2\rangle
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            which is equivalent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} m
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \m\circ f_1=m\circ g_1
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_2=g_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p Summing up we get:
          #sufficient_contractinjective.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              A weakly cohesive topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              is sufficiently cohesive iff all injective objects are contractible.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ▪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %h2#cohesively_connected_truth Cohesively connected truth
          %p
            In a sufficiently cohesive topos the subobject classifier is obviously connected since
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega=\Omega^1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            is connected iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            is contractible.
          %p
            Before embarking on a proof let us consider two non-examples that display varied behaviors in respect to the
            %em connectedness
            of the subobject classifier and the
            %em exactness
            properties of the components functor
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sierpinski+topos"} Sierpinski topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^\to
              is weakly cohesive over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              since there exists a string of adjoint functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Π
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L\dashv\Pi\dashv\Delta\dashv\Gamma\dashv B: Set\to Set^\to
              with
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi ∅
                        %mo →
                        %mi Z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(Z)=\emptyset \to Z
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Π
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X\to Y) = Y
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi Z
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mi id
                        %mi Z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(Z)=Z\overset{id}{\to} Z
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma (X\to Y) = X
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi Z
                        %mo →
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(Z)=Z\to 1
                  \.
            %p
              The Nullstellensatz fails as does the continuity principle. As a right adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
              preserves all limits and the terminal object in particular whence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              is connected in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^\to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
              satisfies the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ore+condition"} Ore condition
              trivially, it follows then from a general result
              %sup#fnref:1
                %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
              of Lawvere that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              is not connected and, accordingly, that the
              %em Sierpinski topos is not sufficiently cohesive!
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              On the other hand, consider the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quiv"} topos of directed graphs
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mo ⇉
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^\rightrightarrows
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^!
              to the section functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_*
              but has a connected components functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quiv#Omega_graph"} subobject classifier for directed graphs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              is connected.
            %p
              Although
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!
              does not preserve finite products in general, it nevertheless preserves finite products with the subobject classifier
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              has arcs between all nodes in both directions. This implies that the proof of proposition
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#homotopy_components"}
              below still goes through and since this is the only argument that hinges on exactness properties of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!
              and nothing hinges on (the existence of) the right adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^!
              at all, the conclusions from
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#connected_truth"}
              hold as well:
              %em
                the subobject classifier of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mo ⇉
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^\rightrightarrows
                is not only connected but contractible!
              In other words, despite not being sufficiently cohesive
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mo ⇉
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^\rightrightarrows
              nevertheless has enough contractible objects.
          %p
            Recall that in any topos, the subobject classifier
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            has two points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi true
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi false
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{true},\mathsf{false}
            fitting into the following pullback diagram (which is an equaliser diagram as well) due to the classifying property of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            for the monomorphism
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\to 1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                            %mi true
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                              %mi false
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  0 &amp;\to &amp; 1
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp; &amp;\downarrow\mathsf{true}
                  \\\
                  1 &amp;\underset{\mathsf{false}}{\to} &amp;\Omega
                  }
          %p
            In a sufficiently cohesive topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            is furthermore connected whence together with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra structure
            we can view it as a generalized (non linear) “interval” object:
          #connector.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              An object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              in a weakly cohesive topos is called a
              %em (cohesive) connector
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(T)=1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              has two points
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_0,t_1:1\to T
              with empty
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equaliser"} equaliser
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi e
                      %mn 1
                      %mover
                        %munder
                          %mo ⇉
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi t
                              %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mn 0
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\overset{e}{\to} 1\overset{t_0}{\underset{t_1}{\rightrightarrows}} T
          %p
            In a topos with a connected subobject classifier
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            itself is a connector. Conversely the existence of a connector implies the connectedness of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
          #connecting_truth.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              be weakly cohesive topos.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              has a connector
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              iff
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(\Omega)=1
          %p
            %strong> Proof
            \. “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            ”: Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi t
                          %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 0
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\overset{t_0}{\underset{t_1}{\rightrightarrows}} T
            be a connector. Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_1:1\to T
            is a subobject with characteristic map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_1:T\to\Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_1\circ t_i
            ,
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i=0,1
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i=1
            this simply yields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi true
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{true}
            by the definition of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_1
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i=0
            we claim that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi false
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_1\circ t_0=\mathsf{false}
            since we have the following diagram:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                            %mi true
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi t
                                %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mi T
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi χ
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  0 &amp;\to &amp; 1 &amp;\to &amp; 1
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp; &amp;t_1\downarrow&amp; &amp; \downarrow \mathsf{true}
                  \\\
                  1&amp;\underset{t_0}{\to} &amp;T&amp;\underset{\chi_1}{\to}&amp;\Omega
                  }
          %p
            Here the left square is a pullback since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi o
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_o,t_1
            have empty equaliser by assumption. The right square is a pullback since it classifies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_1
            whence the outer square is a pullback too. Therefore
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_1\circ t_0
            classifies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\to 1
            which is exactly the definition of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi false
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{false}
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(T)
            is terminal
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(t_0)=p_!(t_1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi true
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi false
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(\mathsf{true})=p_!(\chi_1\circ t_1)=p_!(\chi_1)\circ p_!(t_1)=p_!(\chi_1)\circ p_!(t_0)=p_!(\chi_1\circ t_0)=p_!(\mathsf{false})\quad
          %p
            This says that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi true
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{true}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi false
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{false}
            are in the same connected component but a lattice whose top and bottom elements are in the same component is necessarily connected.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p One can use connectors to define a (generalized) homotopy relation between maps that behaves well under taking connected components.
          #I-homotopy.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              be a connector. Two parallel maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g:A\to B
              are called
              = succeed "," do
                %em I-homotopic
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %msub
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi I
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\sim_I g
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:A\times I\to B
              with the property that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                    %mi and
                    %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                    %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f=h\circ\langle id_A, t_0\circ !_A\rangle\quad and\quad g=h\circ\langle id_A, t_1\circ !_A\rangle\quad.
            %p
              (In this case,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
              is also called an (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              \-)homotopy between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              and
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
          %p
            The following result brings together two ingredients for the equivalence between contractability and connectedness of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(\Omega)=1
          #homotopy_components.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f=h\circ\langle i, k_1\rangle
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g=h\circ\langle i, k_2\rangle
              be a pair of parallel maps in a weakly cohesive topos with the property that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(k_1)=p_!(k_2)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(f)=p_!(g)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi I
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\sim_I g
              implies
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(f)=p_!(g)
          %p
            %strong> Proof
            \. Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!
            preserves finite products it maps product diagrams to product diagrams whence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi j
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(h\circ\langle i, k_j\rangle)=p_!(h)\circ p_!(\langle i, k_j\rangle)=p_!(h)\circ \langle p_!( i), p_!(k_j)\rangle\quad,\quad j\in\{1,2\}
          %p
            but these two maps coincide since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(k_1)=p_!(k_2)
            by assumption.
          %p
            Since for an I-homotopy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi j
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_j=t_j\circ !_A:A\to I
            and,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(I)=1
            by assumption,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(k_j):p_!(A)\to 1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j\in\{1,2\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is terminal whence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(t_0\circ !_A)=p_!(t_1\circ !_A)
            whence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(f)=p_!(g)
            as claimed.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p
            For the following the monoid structure of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            will become important. So let us briefly review the basics:
          %p
            In a general topos, the Heyting algebra structure endows the subobject classifier with the structure of an
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+monoid"} internal monoid
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Ω
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∧
                  %mi Ω
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mtext and the unit by
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi true
                  %mi Ω
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega\times\Omega\overset{\wedge}{\to}\Omega\quad ,
                  \\text{ and the unit by }\quad 1\overset{\mathsf{true}}{\to}\Omega\quad .
          %p
            The conjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            is defined as the characteristic map of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi true
                        %mo ,
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi true
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\xrightarrow{\langle\mathsf{true},\mathsf{true}\rangle}\Omega\times\Omega
          %p
            Importantly, the other truth value
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi false
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\xrightarrow{\mathsf{false}}\Omega
            plays the role of a (multiplicative)
            %em zero
            with respect to this multiplication.
          %p For the following we need
          #omega_homotopy.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_6 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              be a weakly cohesive topos whose subobject classifier is a connector i.e.
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(\Omega)=1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo :
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge :\Omega\times\Omega{\to}\Omega
              is an
              = succeed "-homotopy" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_{\Omega}
              to the constant map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi false
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mo !
                        %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{false}\circ !_\Omega
          %p
            %strong> Proof
            \. We have to show that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi Ω
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi true
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mo !
                    %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge\circ\langle id_\Omega, \mathsf{true}\circ !_\Omega\rangle=id_\Omega
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi Ω
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi false
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mo !
                      %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi false
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mo !
                      %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge\circ\langle id_\Omega, \mathsf{false}\circ !_\Omega\rangle=\mathsf{false}\circ !_\Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            but let us spell out the details diagrammatically:
          %p For the first equation, consider the commutative diagram:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mrow
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                              %mi true
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                                %mi true
                              %mo ,
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                                %mi true
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %msub
                                %mi id
                                %mi Ω
                              %mo ,
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                                %mi true
                              %mo ∘
                              %msub
                                %mo !
                                %mi Ω
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo ×
                          %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  1 &amp;\to &amp; 1
                  \\\
                  {}_\mathsf{true}\downarrow &amp; &amp;\downarrow_{\langle\mathsf{true},\mathsf{true}\rangle}
                  \\\
                  \\Omega &amp;\xrightarrow{\langle id_\Omega,\mathsf{true}\circ !_\Omega\rangle}&amp;\Omega\times\Omega
                  }
          %p
            This pullback pasted to the classifying pullback diagram for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi true
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi true
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle\mathsf{true},\mathsf{true}\rangle
            displays
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi Ω
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi true
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mo !
                    %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge\circ\langle id_\Omega, \mathsf{true}\circ !_\Omega\rangle
            as the characteristic map of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi true
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{true}
            which of course is none other than
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_\Omega
          %p For the second, consider the following pullback:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mo !
                                %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                                %mi true
                              %mo ∘
                              %msub
                                %mo !
                                %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                                %mi true
                              %mo ,
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                                %mi true
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %msub
                                %mi id
                                %mi Ω
                              %mo ,
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                                %mi false
                              %mo ∘
                              %msub
                                %mo !
                                %mi Ω
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo ×
                          %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  X &amp;\xrightarrow{!_X} &amp; 1
                  \\\
                  {}_{\mathsf{true}\circ !_X}\downarrow &amp; &amp;\downarrow_{\langle\mathsf{true},\mathsf{true}\rangle}
                  \\\
                  \\Omega &amp;\xrightarrow{\langle id_\Omega,\mathsf{false}\circ !_\Omega\rangle }&amp;\Omega\times\Omega
                  }
          %p Chasing the arrows around in the second component yields the equation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi true
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mo !
                    %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi false
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mo !
                    %mi Ω
                  %mo ∘
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi true
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mo !
                    %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi false
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mo !
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{true}\circ !_X=\mathsf{false}\circ !_\Omega\circ\mathsf{true}\circ !_X=\mathsf{false}\circ !_X
          %p
            but this implies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X=0
            since it corresponds to the pullback of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi true
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{true}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi false
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{false}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mo !
                                %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                                %mi true
                              %mo ,
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                                %mi true
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                              %mi true
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %msub
                                %mi id
                                %mi Ω
                              %mo ,
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                                %mi false
                              %mo ∘
                              %msub
                                %mo !
                                %mi Ω
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo ×
                          %mi Ω
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mo ∧
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  0 &amp;\xrightarrow{!_0} &amp; 1 &amp;\to &amp; 1
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp; &amp;\downarrow_{\langle\mathsf{true},\mathsf{true}\rangle}&amp; &amp;\downarrow _\mathsf{true}
                  \\\
                  \\Omega &amp;\xrightarrow{\langle id_\Omega,\mathsf{false}\circ !_\Omega\rangle }&amp;\Omega\times\Omega&amp;\xrightarrow{\wedge}&amp;\Omega
                  }
          %p
            is the classifying pullback of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ↣
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\rightarrowtail\Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi false
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mo !
                    %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{false}\circ !_\Omega
            is also the characteristic map of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ↣
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\rightarrowtail\Omega
            the claim follows.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p
            In order to show that the connectedness of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            implies its contractibility we will now lift this
            = succeed "-homotopy" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_\Omega
            and a constant map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega\to\Omega
            to a
            = succeed "-homotopy" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_{\Omega^X}
            and a constant map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^X\to\Omega^X
          #homotopy_lifting.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_7 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              be a weakly cohesive topos with connector
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mover
                        %munder
                          %mo ⇉
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi t
                              %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mn 0
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\overset{t_0}{\underset{t_1}{\rightrightarrows}} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m:X\times T\to X
              is a
              = succeed "-homotopy" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_X
              and a constant map
              = succeed ".Then" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c:X\to X\,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              a
              = succeed "-homotopy" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi Y
                    %mo ×
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu:X^Y\times T\to X^Y
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi X
                          %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_{X^Y}
              to a constant endomap
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^Y\to X^Y
          %p
            %strong> Proof
            \. We get
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi T
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi m
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times T \overset{m}{\to}  X
            as follows
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mi T
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mtext by transposal
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mi T
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi Y
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %msup
                            %mtext by application of the endofunctor (-)
                            %mi Y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mi Y
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi T
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mtext by using rules for powers
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mi Y
                          %mo ×
                          %mi T
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi μ
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mi Y
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mtext by reversing transposal .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  X&amp; \to X^{T}\quad\text{by transposal}
                  \\\
                  X^Y&amp;\to (X^{T})^{Y}\quad\text{by application of the endofunctor (-)}^Y
                  \\\
                  X^Y &amp;\to (X^Y)^{T}\quad\text{by using rules for powers}
                  \\\
                  X^Y\times T&amp;\overset{\mu}{\to}X^Y\quad \text{by reversing transposal .}
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            The corresponding terms in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            are
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi m
                          %mo =
                          %mi λ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo .
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi λ
                          %mi x
                          %mi λ
                          %mi t
                          %mo .
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi λ
                          %mi f
                          %mi λ
                          %mi y
                          %mi λ
                          %mi t
                          %mo .
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi λ
                          %mi f
                          %mi λ
                          %mi t
                          %mi λ
                          %mi y
                          %mo .
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi μ
                          %mo =
                          %mi λ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi f
                          %mo ,
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi λ
                          %mi y
                          %mo .
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  m=\lambda (x,t).m(x,t)
                  \\\
                  \\lambda x\lambda t.m(x,t)
                  \\\
                  \\lambda f\lambda y\lambda t. m(f(y),t)
                  \\\
                  \\lambda f\lambda t \lambda y. m(f(y),t)
                  \\\
                  \\mu =\lambda (f,t)\lambda y. m(f(y),t)
                  }
          %p
            respectively. The last term evaluates to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mi f
                  %mi λ
                  %mi y
                  %mo .
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda f\lambda y.f(y)
            i.e. to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_{X^Y}
            at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_0
            and at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_1
            evaluates to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mi f
                  %mi λ
                  %mi y
                  %mo .
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda f\lambda y.c
            , i.e. the function mapping f to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^Y
            , the function constantly
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is a
            = succeed "-homotopy" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_{X^Y}
            to the constantly
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^Y
            map.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p
            By prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#omega_homotopy"}
            and the preceding the next is immediate:
          #omega_action.num_prop
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              be a weakly cohesive topos whose subobject classifier is a connector. The
              = succeed "-homotopy" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo :
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge :\Omega\times\Omega{\to}\Omega
              lifts to a
              = succeed "-homotopy" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_{\Omega^X}
              and a constant map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^X\to\Omega^X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ▪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              In classical
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with the property that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_X
              is homotopic to a constant map is called a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+space"} contractible space
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#homotopy_lifting"}
              can be viewed as a synthetic internal avatar of the classical fact, that any two parallel maps into a contractible space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              are homotopic.
            %p
              Pursuing this analogy, the preceding result shows that in a weakly cohesive topos with connected subobject classifier, the power object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^X
              of an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is akin to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone"} cone
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}X
              of a topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              in providing a contractible object to embed into. In this perspective, one can think of a sufficiently cohesive topos as being equipped with a generalized cone construction.
          #connected_truth.num_prop
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              be a weakly cohesive topos. Then the subobject classifier
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              is connected iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              is contractible. In other words,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              is sufficiently cohesive iff
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(\Omega)=1
          %p
            %strong> Proof
            \. “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            ”: The propositions
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#omega_action"}
            and
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#homotopy_components"}
            imply that for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(id_{\Omega^X})=id_{p_!(\Omega^X)}
            is a constant map. By observation
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#constant_homotopy"}
            it then follows that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(\Omega^X)=1
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            which is just the definition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            being contractible.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p For convenience and summary let us collect all the equivalent formulations of sufficient cohesion in one place:
          #sufficient_cohesion_equivalences.num_prop
            %h6#theorem_lawvere Theorem (Lawvere)
            %p
              A weakly cohesive topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              is sufficiently cohesive iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              satisfies the following equivalent conditions:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℰ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega\in\mathcal{E}
                  is contractible i.e.
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mo !
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(\Omega^X)=1
                  for every object
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi ℰ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in\mathcal{E}
                  = succeed "\u2019" do
                    %em truth is contractible
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℰ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega\in\mathcal{E}
                  is connected i.e.
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi p
                            %mo !
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!(\Omega)=1
                  = succeed "\u2019" do
                    %em truth is connected
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℰ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega\in\mathcal{E}
                  is a connector. ‘
                  %em> truth is a connector
                  ’
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℰ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
                  has a connector.
              %li
                %p
                  Every object
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℰ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in\mathcal{E}
                  embeds into a contractible object. ‘
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℰ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
                  = succeed "\u2019" do
                    %em has enough contractible objects
              %li
                %p
                  Every object
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℰ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in\mathcal{E}
                  embeds into a connected object. ‘
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℰ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
                  = succeed "\u2019" do
                    %em has enough connected objects
              %li
                %p All injective objects are connected.
              %li
                %p
                  All injective objects are contractible.
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ▪
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
